(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-pd2-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"PD2 Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What causes SENILE ptosis?","choices":["Interruption of the sympathetic supply","Compression of the oculomotor nerve","Weakened muscle, relaxed tissues","Antibody blockade at the neuromuscular junction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — senile ptosis comes from weakened muscle and relaxed tissues, plus the weight of herniated fat. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why is natural light preferred for inspecting the skin?","choices":["Artificial light makes lesion borders appear larger than they are","Natural light reduces the glare produced by moist lesions","Artificial light may distort skin tone","Natural light is brighter than any artificial source available in clinic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the distortion can conceal jaundice, pallor and subtle erythema. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Besides Horner syndrome and the senile and congenital forms, which causes of ptosis remain?","choices":["Bell palsy, trigeminal neuralgia and stroke","Thyroid eye disease, orbital cellulitis and tumor","Blepharitis, chalazion and dermatochalasis","Myasthenia gravis, oculomotor nerve damage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ptosis also comes from myasthenia gravis and oculomotor nerve damage, alongside Horner syndrome's sympathetic loss. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which description fits a typical tongue cancer?","choices":["On the dorsum, as smooth red patches, in females under thirty","At the tip, as painful ulcers, in any age group","On the undersurface, as blue tortuous swellings","On the lateral border or undersurface, as an indurated lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tongue cancers are usually indurated red or white lesions on the lateral border or undersurface, typically in males over 50. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which medication history should be taken specifically when a patient reports hearing loss?","choices":["Oral contraceptives and hormone replacement","Aminoglycosides","Beta blockers, statins and proton pump inhibitors","Inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting bronchodilators"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides are ototoxic, as are aspirin, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, quinine and furosemide, so each is asked about by name. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Generalized itching with no obvious skin lesion should prompt consideration of which causes?","choices":["Contact dermatitis and scabies exclusively","Anxiety and stress, once organic causes have been excluded","Dry skin, aging, pregnancy, uremia, jaundice, lymphomas, leukemias, drug reaction and lice","Dry skin and aging only, since these account for nearly all cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the list spans dermatological, systemic, malignant and infestational causes. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are the pupil findings in Adie's tonic pupil?","choices":["Small, unequal and irregular; no light reaction but constricts on accommodation","Small but briskly reactive, with ptosis and anhidrosis","Dilated and fixed to both light and near, with ptosis and lateral deviation","Large, regular, usually unilateral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Adie's pupil is large, regular and usually unilateral, with a reduced light reaction and a very slow near reaction. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the Rinne result in SENSORINEURAL hearing loss?","choices":["The result is unpredictable in sensorineural loss","Air conduction still greater than bone conduction","Bone conduction greater than air conduction, because the inner ear is damaged","Neither route is heard"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Air conduction stays longer than bone conduction and the normal pattern prevails, because the inner ear or cochlear nerve fails however the vibrations arrive. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which two symptoms together suggest an allergic cause?","choices":["Deep pain and photophobia","Burning and purulent discharge","Foreign body sensation and reduced vision","Itching and excessive tearing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — itching together with excessive tearing suggests an allergic cause of the eye complaint. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A smooth, beefy red tongue points to which deficiency?","choices":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Iron alone","Vitamin D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The appearance is atrophic glossitis, and it earns a look for the rest of the deficiency picture. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which part of the hand should be used to assess skin temperature?","choices":["The ulnar border","The dorsal aspect","The palmar aspect","The fingertips"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the back of the hand is more sensitive to temperature than the palm. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Eye pain ON EYE MOTION suggests what?","choices":["Conjunctivitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Corneal abrasion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — eye pain that comes with eye motion is the pairing for optic neuritis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which statement correctly sorts migraine, tension and cluster headaches by their EPISODIC and UNILATERAL patterns?","choices":["Migraine and cluster are episodic; migraine and tension are unilateral","All three are episodic and all three unilateral","Only cluster is episodic and only tension unilateral","Migraine and tension are episodic; migraine and cluster are unilateral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Migraine appears in both lists, which is why the two features have to be taken together rather than either alone. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What does tracheal DEVIATION suggest?","choices":["Thyroid nodules only","Lymphadenopathy","Ludwig's angina","Masses, atelectasis or a large pneumothorax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Something is either pulling the trachea toward it or pushing it away. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Eye pain with PHOTOPHOBIA suggests what?","choices":["Inflammation of the iris","Optic neuritis","Temporal arteritis","Conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — eye pain accompanied by photophobia is the pairing for inflammation of the iris. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is trichiasis?","choices":["Inward turning of the lower lid margin","Outward turning of the lower lid margin","Drooping of the upper lid","Posteriorly misdirected eyelashes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — trichiasis is posteriorly misdirected eyelashes, a lid finding separate from entropion and ectropion. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"VERTICAL diplopia — images on top of each other — points to a palsy of which cranial nerves?","choices":["Third or sixth","Fourth or sixth","Second or third","Third or fourth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — vertical diplopia, with images on top of each other, points to a palsy of cranial nerve III or IV. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is the whispered voice test set up?","choices":["Stand beside the patient and whisper into both ears at once","Stand two feet behind the patient, occlude the ear not being tested","Stand two feet in front of the patient so they can see your mouth","Stand across the room and speak at conversational volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The examiner stands two feet behind the patient and occludes the ear not being tested, then whispers a three-item sequence twice. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How should psychological stress be regarded in a patient with a skin disorder?","choices":["It is the single commonest cause of chronic skin disease","It has no demonstrable effect on the course of skin disease","It should be raised only after organic causes have been excluded","It is seldom the sole cause, but it can exacerbate many dermatoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the distinction between causing and worsening is the point being made. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Besides the whispered voice, which two bedside hearing screens are used?","choices":["Weber and Rinne","Finger rub and a watch","Tuning fork and audiometry","Otoscopy and insufflation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both are crude but immediately available, and both test whether any sound is heard at all. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Submandibular swelling and erythema with cellulitis of the floor of the mouth. Which description fits?","choices":["A peritonsillar abscess — the danger is rupture","A submandibular stone — the danger is infection","Ludwig's angina — it is life threatening because of the AIRWAY","Ludwig's angina — the danger is spread to the mediastinum only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The swelling pushes the tongue and floor of mouth upward and back, and the airway is the reason this cannot wait. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which chain comes FIRST in the standard sequence for palpating the neck nodes?","choices":["Preauricular","Supraclavicular","Submental","Deep cervical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sequence runs preauricular, postauricular, occipital, tonsillar, submandibular, submental, superficial cervical, posterior cervical, deep cervical, supraclavicular — following a route so none is missed. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which two conditions are associated with blepharitis?","choices":["Graves disease and myasthenia gravis","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus","Bacterial infection and atopic dermatitis","Hypothyroidism and iron deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — blepharitis is a common eye inflammation associated with bacterial infections and with atopic dermatitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In a trauma patient, what must NOT be done?","choices":["Do not measure visual acuity","Do not inspect the lids","Do not take a history","Do not palpate the globe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — in trauma it is critical not to palpate the globe, and too much pressure on a ruptured globe is a known mistake. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Watery or mucoid discharge suggests which conditions?","choices":["Acute iritis","Acute glaucoma","Allergic or viral","Bacterial infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — watery or mucoid discharge goes with allergic or viral conditions, while purulent discharge suggests bacteria. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the Centor rule used for?","choices":["Predicting which patients need tonsillectomy","Deciding, from clinical features, how likely a sore throat is to be bacterial","Grading the severity of tonsillar enlargement","Distinguishing peritonsillar abscess from tonsillitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a clinical prediction rule — it converts findings into a probability and a course of action. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What does reduced mobility of the tympanic membrane on insufflation indicate?","choices":["Sensorineural hearing loss","Effusion behind the membrane","Perforation of the membrane","Cerumen impaction in the canal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reduced mobility on insufflation points to an effusion behind the membrane or to a thickened tympanic membrane. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A brown to black discoloration of the nail should raise concern for which diagnosis?","choices":["Melanoma","Pseudomonas infection","Iron deficiency","Psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pigmented change in a nail warrants the same concern as a pigmented skin lesion. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"How should physical examination findings be recorded in a written clinical note?","choices":["Assign each system a numeric score on a standard scale","Describe what was found, rather than writing normal, abnormal or unremarkable","Use unremarkable wherever nothing is wrong, to keep the note concise","Record only the abnormal findings and omit the normal ones entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a description communicates what was actually observed, where a label does not. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"Which primary lesion usually precedes a crust?","choices":["A wheal","A nodule or tumor","A vesicle, bulla or pustule","A macule or patch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — each of the three ruptures and leaves dried serum, blood and debris behind. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Purulent discharge suggests what?","choices":["Bacterial infection","Allergic conjunctivitis","Viral conjunctivitis","Dry eye"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — purulent discharge suggests a bacterial infection, while watery or mucoid discharge suggests allergy or a virus. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Gradual, painless visual loss suggests what?","choices":["Simple glaucoma or cataract","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Vitreous hemorrhage","Chemical exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — gradual, painless visual loss suggests simple glaucoma or cataracts. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do you assess whether the eyes are truly protruding?","choices":["Stand behind the seated patient and inspect from above","Sit facing the patient and compare the two eyes directly","Shine a light from the temporal side and look for a shadow","Palpate the globe to judge its forward position"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — stand behind the seated patient, look down from above, and draw the lids up to compare the corneas with the lower lids. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is the auricle positioned to straighten the ear canal for otoscopy in an adult?","choices":["Leave it undisturbed and advance the speculum slowly","Pull it up, back, and away from the head","Pull it down and back","Press it forward against the cheek"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all three directions. The canal is not straight, and the view is only as good as the alignment. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which malignancy is the most frequent neoplasm occurring in patients with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Kaposi's sarcoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lesions start light colored and coalesce into darker blue-purple macules, papules, nodules and plaques. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How does the RATE of onset of visual impairment guide the differential?","choices":["Rate of onset does not narrow the differential","Rapid deterioration always means trauma","Rapid deterioration suggests a vascular cause","Rapid deterioration suggests cataract; gradual loss suggests a vascular cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — rapid deterioration should make you strongly consider vascular causes, while gradual loss suggests causes such as cataract. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Psoriasis characteristically appears in which distribution?","choices":["Dermatomal","Extensor","Flexor","Photodistributed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the elbows and knees are the classic extensor sites. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"When a third party supplies the patient's responses during an assessed encounter, where should your attention stay?","choices":["Divided evenly between the patient and the person answering","On the patient, interacting with the patient rather than the person supplying the answers","On whoever is supplying the answers, since that is the source of information","On the documentation form, so that responses are recorded accurately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — you should always be looking at and interacting with the patient, even when the responses are supplied by someone else. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 8"},{"q":"In the melanoma warning signs, what does D stand for?","choices":["Darkening of the surrounding skin","Diameter larger than six millimeters","Depth of invasion into the dermis","Duration of more than six months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — six millimeters is roughly the diameter of a pencil eraser. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is nystagmus?","choices":["Fine rhythmic oscillation of the eyes","Misalignment of the visual axes — a few degrees is normal","Drooping of the upper lid — a few millimeters is normal","Unequal pupil size — a few millimeters is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nystagmus is fine rhythmic oscillation of the eyes, and a few beats on lateral gaze are normal. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Why might a patient with conductive hearing loss say they hear BETTER in a noisy room?","choices":["The patient compensates by lip reading, which is easier in company","The conductive block attenuates the background noise along with everything else","Noise stimulates the cochlear hair cells directly","Conductive loss spares the high frequencies that carry noise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Others raise their voices over the noise while the patient's block filters the noise itself, so the speech-to-noise ratio improves. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which glands become active during puberty and secrete pheromones?","choices":["Apocrine glands","Eccrine glands","Sebaceous glands","Meibomian glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Their activation at puberty is what distinguishes them from eccrine glands. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Nasal polyps are associated with which conditions?","choices":["Asthma, aspirin sensitivity and cystic fibrosis","Hypertension, anticoagulation and atherosclerosis","Smoking, alcohol and reflux","Diabetes, obesity and sleep apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nasal polyps are associated with allergic rhinitis, aspirin sensitivity, asthma, chronic sinus infection and cystic fibrosis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which part of the hand is used to palpate the lymph nodes of the neck?","choices":["The fingertips of all four fingers","The thumb and index finger in a pincer grip","The ulnar border of the hand","The pads of the index and middle fingers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pads are the most sensitive surface, and two fingers cover a node without rolling past it. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which two structures are commonly left unexamined?","choices":["The eyelids and eyebrows","The lacrimal sac and puncta","The extraocular muscles","The cornea and the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — failure to examine the cornea and lens is one of the common mistakes in the eye examination. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Alopecia areata is associated with which category of disease?","choices":["Psychiatric disorders","Endocrine disorders of androgen excess","Autoimmune disorders","Fungal infections"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is a chronic inflammatory disease of the hair follicles. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What does the term hirsutism describe?","choices":["Increased hair growth in women following a male pattern of distribution","Diffuse thinning of hair in women after menopause","Loss of hair in multiple round patches","Excessive fine hair covering the whole body in infancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the pattern of distribution, not simply the amount, defines the term. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which of these belongs in the URGENT group, seen by ophthalmology within a day or less?","choices":["Dry eye and blepharitis","Pinguecula and pterygium","Acute glaucoma","Sudden vision loss, chemical burns and globe rupture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — acute glaucoma is on the urgent list with orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment and hyphema. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which processes can convert a primary lesion into a secondary one?","choices":["Only the passage of time, independent of any external factor","Only secondary bacterial infection of the original lesion","Disease progression, treatment, and manipulation such as picking or scratching","Disease progression alone, since treatment does not alter lesion morphology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the patient's own handling of a lesion is as capable of changing it as the disease itself. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does UNILATERAL nasal obstruction suggest?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis","Viral rhinitis","Rhinitis medicamentosa","A foreign body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unilateral obstruction suggests a foreign body, a tumor or a deviated septum, all structural causes on the blocked side. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A purple lesion does not fade when a glass slide is pressed against it. What does this indicate?","choices":["Inadequate peripheral circulation","Hemorrhage in the skin","Vascular engorgement","An allergic reaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — blood outside the vessels cannot be displaced by pressure, which is why petechiae and purpura do not blanch. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"You lift a fold of skin and release it, and the skin remains elevated. What does this indicate?","choices":["Lichenification","Scleroderma","Dehydration","Edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal skin resumes its shape quickly; skin that stays tented has lost turgor. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Cracking and inflammation at the CORNERS of the mouth. What is this called?","choices":["Angioedema","Herpes simplex","Aphthous ulceration","Angular cheilitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — one of three lip abnormalities to recognize, alongside angioedema and herpes simplex. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A linear crack in the skin, as seen in athlete's foot, is called what?","choices":["A fissure","An erosion","An excoriation","A scale"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the interdigital spaces of the foot are the classic site. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Bilateral visual loss usually implies which kind of problem?","choices":["A primary neurologic cause rather than a primary ophthalmologic one","A primary ophthalmologic cause","An infective cause","A traumatic cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bilateral visual loss usually implies a primary neurologic etiology and not a primary ophthalmologic problem. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"A sore AND SMOOTH tongue suggests what?","choices":["Geographic tongue","Oral candidiasis","Nutritional deficiency","Aphthous ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The smoothness is loss of papillae, and that combination of sore and smooth is the pointer. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Firm stroking of a patient's skin produces a red line, a broadening flare, and then a linear wheal. What is this called?","choices":["The triple response of Lewis","The diascopy response","An excoriation","Lichenification"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — capillary dilatation, arteriolar dilatation and then transudation of fluid, in that sequence. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which hair characteristics are assessed by inspection rather than palpation?","choices":["All four characteristics by inspection alone","All four characteristics by palpation alone","Color, distribution and quantity by inspection","Texture by inspection; color and quantity by palpation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — color, distribution and quantity are inspected, leaving texture as the one hair characteristic assessed by palpation. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"You see a patient whose skin, hair and nail hygiene appears inadequate. What should this prompt you to assess?","choices":["The social history","Whether the patient is being truthful about how often they bathe","Nutritional status through serum albumin and prealbumin levels","Whether the patient has access to running water at home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with cognitive problems may not attend to hygiene measures, so poor hygiene is a clinical finding worth explaining. Cognition and ability to perform activities of daily living. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
